{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"unrational-fusible-godwit","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# Cloud's acli_graph whole-project readers spin forever on a repeated cursor token\n\n## Mechanism (proven, not inferred)\n\n`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira/acli_graph.py` — all three whole-project\nreaders (`get_parent_map` :215-278, `get_comment_map` :318-376, `get_issuelinks_map`\n:412-473) walk `POST /rest/api/3/search/jql` with this loop shape:\n\n    while True:\n        ... resp = self._direct_rest_post_json(\"/rest/api/3/search/jql\", body)\n        if resp.get(\"isLast\"): break\n        next_page_token = resp.get(\"nextPageToken\")\n        if not next_page_token: break\n\nIf the server returns the SAME non-null `nextPageToken` on consecutive responses with\n`isLast` falsy, the request body is byte-identical each iteration, the response is\nidentical, and neither break fires: the loop never terminates. There is no\nsame-token-twice guard and no request-count ceiling.\n\n`fetcher._iter_pages` has exactly this guard (`fetcher.py:286-308`, `token_seen_count` ->\n`SilentTruncationError(reason=\"same-token-twice\")`); these three readers, a separate code\npath with a separate cursor, do not.\n\nSecond-order effect: because each reader wraps the POST in a fail-open\n`except Exception: ... break`, the spin ends only when the transport itself finally\nerrors, and that error is then swallowed at WARNING — so the pass completes with a\nSILENTLY PARTIAL map (missing parents / comments / links), which is the same invisible\nsymptom as the truncation bugs in change 1105, [rebar:9263-b404-cb6c-463f] and\n[rebar:deac-4f5a-856a-49bd].\n\n## Reproduction\n\nSubstitute `_direct_rest_post_json` with a stub returning\n`{\"issues\": [...], \"nextPageToken\": \"same-token-forever\"}` (no `isLast`). Measured on\nthis branch: 26 requests issued, ending only because the harness raised at its own\n25-call cap; result was a 1-entry map. Without the harness cap the call does not return.\n\nThe oracle for the fixed behaviour already exists, as a strict xfail:\n`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_acli_graph_pagination_heldout.py`\n`::test_a_repeated_cursor_token_stops_the_walk_on_its_own`. It asserts the reader stops\non its own within two requests. When the guard lands the xfail turns strict-red and must\nbe converted to a plain assertion.\n\n## Why it was not fixed under 0fad\n\n0fad is a test-parity ticket (its ACs are tests + a structural guard); its instructions\nscoped production changes out. This ticket carries the production fix.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] the three `acli_graph` readers stop on a repeated cursor token instead of spinning\n      — preferably by routing all three through one shared cursor pager rather than a\n      fourth hand-rolled loop\n- [ ] a stalled cursor is LOUD, matching `fetcher._iter_pages`: raise\n      `SilentTruncationError(reason=\"same-token-twice\")` rather than returning a partial\n      map, or state on this ticket why a silent stop is correct for these readers\n- [ ] the fail-open `except Exception` no longer converts a stalled cursor into a silently\n      partial whole-project map\n- [ ] `test_a_repeated_cursor_token_stops_the_walk_on_its_own` is converted from\n      `xfail(strict=True)` to a plain passing assertion","id":"cabc-7a98-d173-4d7c","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Cloud acli_graph readers spin forever on a repeated cursor token"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785720095826313001,"uuid":"3bba509a-f059-4206-a0bc-076a8dfd4d61"}